  "text": "Every product team keeps a chart like this one. Each row is the users who first showed up in one month. Each cell is the share of them still active a month later, then two, then three, so every row steps down. Building it is a classic data science SQL question, and each of its pieces gets asked on its own. The page asks fifteen questions. Six get a real query here. The other nine come quickly at the end, since the data analyst videos take them apart in depth. The first piece: who came back at all.\n\nQuestion one. Inner join or left join: which does a retention query need? Here are January's six active users, and February's four. An inner join keeps only the users found in both months: three rows. Count February's matches over those rows, and retention comes out at a hundred percent. The users who left were dropped before anything was counted. A left join keeps all six January users and writes null where February has no match. Three out of six came back: fifty percent. Question two. How do you find users active one month but not the next? Those nulls are the answer. Keep the left join, and filter where February's user ID is null: users one, four and six. Not exists returns the same three. Retention is a rate, and every rate has a denominator that can be zero.",
